{"st":"MI","name":"Michigan","seats":[{"census":1830,"seats":1},{"census":1840,"seats":3},{"census":1850,"seats":4},{"census":1860,"seats":6},{"census":1870,"seats":9},{"census":1880,"seats":11},{"census":1890,"seats":12},{"census":1900,"seats":12},{"census":1910,"seats":13},{"census":1920,"seats":13},{"census":1930,"seats":17},{"census":1940,"seats":17},{"census":1950,"seats":18},{"census":1960,"seats":19},{"census":1970,"seats":19},{"census":1980,"seats":18},{"census":1990,"seats":16},{"census":2000,"seats":15},{"census":2010,"seats":14},{"census":2020,"seats":13}],"events":[{"congress":24,"year":1837,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Michigan became the 26th state on January 26, 1837. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 1 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 3 to 4 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 4 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 6 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 9 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 11 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Michigan kept 12 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 12 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 63rd Congress (1913–1915), Michigan elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 13 to 17 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Michigan kept 17 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 17 to 18 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Michigan's House delegation grew from 18 to 19 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 88th Congress (1963–1965), Michigan elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Michigan kept 19 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Michigan's House delegation shrank from 19 to 18 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Michigan's House delegation shrank from 18 to 16 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"court","title":"Good v. Austin","text":"For the third decade in a row, the legislature did not pass a congressional plan, so a three-judge federal court set Michigan's districts in 1992, as the state fell from 18 seats to 16.","sources":["https://www.leagle.com/decision/19921357800fsupp55711303"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Michigan's House delegation shrank from 16 to 15 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Michigan's House delegation shrank from 15 to 14 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":115,"year":2018,"kind":"other","title":"Citizens commission created","text":"Michigan voters approved Proposal 2, creating the Independent Citizens Redistricting Commission. Its first congressional map was adopted in December 2021 and used from the 2022 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/michigan/","https://ballotpedia.org/Michigan_Proposal_2,_Independent_Redistricting_Commission_Initiative_(2018)"]},{"congress":116,"year":2019,"kind":"court","title":"League of Women Voters v. Benson","text":"In April 2019 a federal court ruled that challenged congressional and legislative districts were partisan gerrymanders and ordered new maps. In October 2019 the Supreme Court vacated that ruling after holding in Rucho v. Common Cause that federal courts cannot decide such claims.","sources":["https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/court-cases/league-women-voters-michigan-v-benson","https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/michigan/"],"mapChange":false},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Michigan's House delegation shrank from 14 to 13 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]}],"compactness":{"c024":{"MI-0":0.083},"c025":{"MI-0":0.083},"c026":{"MI-0":0.083},"c027":{"MI-0":0.083},"c028":{"MI-1":0.427,"MI-2":0.419,"MI-3":0.065},"c029":{"MI-1":0.427,"MI-2":0.419,"MI-3":0.065},"c030":{"MI-1":0.427,"MI-2":0.419,"MI-3":0.065},"c031":{"MI-1":0.427,"MI-2":0.419,"MI-3":0.065},"c032":{"MI-1":0.427,"MI-2":0.419,"MI-3":0.065},"c033":{"MI-1":0.36,"MI-2":0.341,"MI-3":0.428,"MI-4":0.062},"c034":{"MI-1":0.36,"MI-2":0.341,"MI-3":0.428,"MI-4":0.062},"c035":{"MI-1":0.36,"MI-2":0.341,"MI-3":0.428,"MI-4":0.062},"c036":{"MI-1":0.36,"MI-2":0.341,"MI-3":0.428,"MI-4":0.062},"c037":{"MI-1":0.36,"MI-2":0.341,"MI-3":0.428,"MI-4":0.062},"c038":{"MI-1":0.33,"MI-2":0.506,"MI-3":0.549,"MI-4":0.084,"MI-5":0.394,"MI-6":0.07},"c039":{"MI-1":0.33,"MI-2":0.506,"MI-3":0.549,"MI-4":0.084,"MI-5":0.394,"MI-6":0.07},"c040":{"MI-1":0.33,"MI-2":0.506,"MI-3":0.549,"MI-4":0.084,"MI-5":0.394,"MI-6":0.07},"c041":{"MI-1":0.33,"MI-2":0.506,"MI-3":0.549,"MI-4":0.084,"MI-5":0.394,"MI-6":0.07},"c042":{"MI-1":0.33,"MI-2":0.506,"MI-3":0.549,"MI-4":0.084,"MI-5":0.394,"MI-6":0.07},"c043":{"MI-1":0.418,"MI-2":0.483,"MI-3":0.486,"MI-4":0.674,"MI-5":0.466,"MI-6":0.588,"MI-7":0.434,"MI-8":0.285,"MI-9":0.069},"c044":{"MI-1":0.418,"MI-2":0.483,"MI-3":0.486,"MI-4":0.674,"MI-5":0.466,"MI-6":0.588,"MI-7":0.434,"MI-8":0.285,"MI-9":0.069},"c045":{"MI-1":0.418,"